==================================================
EXPERIMENT 1
CONTAINERIZE AND RUN A SIMPLE PYTHON WEB APPLICATION
==================================================


====================
FILE 1: app.py
====================

from flask import Flask

app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route("/")
def home():
    return "Hello! My first Docker application is running."

app.run(host="0.0.0.0", port=5000)


====================
FILE 2: requirements.txt
====================

flask


====================
FILE 3: Dockerfile
====================

FROM python:3.12-slim

WORKDIR /app

COPY requirements.txt .

RUN pip install -r requirements.txt

COPY app.py .

EXPOSE 5000

CMD ["python", "app.py"]


====================
DOCKER COMMANDS
====================

1. Check Docker version

docker --version


2. Test Docker

docker run hello-world


3. Go to Desktop

cd Desktop


4. Create project folder

mkdir docker-python-app


5. Enter project folder

cd docker-python-app


6. Check files

dir


7. Build Docker image

docker build -t my-python-app .


8. Check Docker images

docker images


9. Run Docker container

docker run -d -p 5000:5000 --name my-python-container my-python-app


10. Check running containers

docker ps


11. Open application in browser

http://localhost:5000


12. View container logs

docker logs my-python-container


13. Stop container

docker stop my-python-container


14. Start container again

docker start my-python-container


15. Check running containers

docker ps


16. Stop container before removing

docker stop my-python-container


17. Remove container

docker rm my-python-container


18. Check all containers

docker ps -a


19. Check Docker images

docker images


